NAME
       swapon, swapoff - start and stop using swap space

SYNOPSIS
       swapon [-p PRIORITY] DEVICE...
       swapon -a
       swapon --show | -s
       swapoff DEVICE...
       swapoff -a

DESCRIPTION
       Swap space is disk space the kernel uses as extra, slow memory: when RAM
       runs short, pages that are rarely used are moved out to swap. A swap
       area is a partition (type "Linux swap"), a logical volume or a file,
       prepared once with mkswap.

       swapon starts using swap areas; swapoff stops using them (the pages in
       them are first moved back into RAM, so there must be room). Neither
       survives a reboot by itself: to use a swap area at every boot, add a
       line to /etc/fstab with the type swap and the mount point none, then
       test it with swapon -a.

OPTIONS
       -a, --all
              swapon: start every swap area listed in /etc/fstab (except those
              marked noauto) that is not active yet. swapoff: stop all swap.

       -p, --priority=N
              Give this area a priority (-1 to 32767). The kernel fills higher
              priorities first. In /etc/fstab write it as the option pri=N.

       --show
              List the active swap areas: name, kind (partition or file), size,
              how much is used and the priority.

       -s, --summary
              A short list of the active swap areas (the same as
              cat /proc/swaps).

       -v, --verbose
              Say what is being done.

       -U UUID, -L LABEL
              Name the swap area by its UUID or label.

EXAMPLES
       Make a new 1 GiB swap partition on /dev/vdb and use it now and at boot:

           parted -s /dev/vdb mkpart swap1 linux-swap 1MiB 1025MiB
           mkswap /dev/vdb1
           blkid /dev/vdb1
           echo 'UUID=<uuid-from-blkid> none swap defaults 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
           swapon -a
           swapon --show

       Use a swap logical volume with a higher priority:

           mkswap /dev/vgdata/swaplv
           echo '/dev/vgdata/swaplv none swap pri=10 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
           swapon -a

       Stop using a swap area before removing it:

           swapoff /dev/vdb1

NOTES
       Checking with swapon -a after editing /etc/fstab catches typing
       mistakes before a reboot does. free -h shows the total swap.

FILES
       /etc/fstab
              Swap areas used at boot.

       /proc/swaps
              The active swap areas.

SEE ALSO
       mkswap(8), fstab(5), free(1), blkid(8), lvcreate(8)
